 calculating the calibration curve. A bigger number requires more\n1217 |             data.\n1218 | \n1219 |         strategy : {'uniform', 'quantile'}, default='uniform'\n1220 |             Strategy used to define the widths of the bins.\n1221 | \n1222 |             - `'uniform'`: The bins have identical widths.\n1223 |             - `'quantile'`: The bins have the same number of samples and depend\n1224 |               on predicted probabilities.\n1225 | \n1226 |         pos_label : int, float, bool or str, default=None\n1227 |             The positive class when computing the calibration curve.\n1228 |             By default, `estimators.classes_[1]` is considered as the\n1229 |             positive class.\n1230 | \n1231 |             .. versionadded:: 1.1\n1232 | \n1233 |         name : str, default=None\n1234 |             Name for labeling curve. If `None`, the name of the estimator is\n1235 |             used.\n1236 | \n1237 |         ref_line : bool, default=True\n1238 |             If `True`, plots a reference line representing a perfectly\n1239 |             calibrated classifier.\n1240 | \n1241 |         ax : matplotlib axes, default=None\n1242 |             Axes object to plot on. If `None`, a new figure and axes is\n1243 |             created.\n1244 | \n1245 |         **kwargs : dict\n1246 |             Keyword arguments to be passed to :func:`matplotlib.pyplot.plot`.\n1247 | \n1248 |         Returns\n1249 |         -------\n1250 |         display : :class:`~sklearn.calibration.CalibrationDisplay`.\n1251 |             Object that stores computed values.\n1252 | \n1253 |         See Also\n1254 |         --------\n1255 |         CalibrationDisplay.from_predictions : Plot calibration curve using true\n1256 |             and predicted labels.\n1257 | \n1258 |         Examples\n1259 |         --------\n1260 |         >>> import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n1261 |         >>> from sklearn.datasets import make_classification\n1262 |         >>> from sklearn.model_selection import train_test_split\n1263 |         >>> from sklearn.linear_model import LogisticRegression\n1264 |         >>> from sklearn.calibration import CalibrationDisplay\n1265 |         >>> X, y = make_classification(random_state=0)\n1266 |         >>> X_train, X_test, y_train, y_test = train_test_split(\n1267 |         ...     X, y, random_state=0)\n1268 |         >>> clf = LogisticRegression(random_state=0)\n1269 |         >>> clf.fit(X_train, y_train)\n1270 |         LogisticRegression(random_state=0)\n1271 |         >>> disp = CalibrationDisplay.from_estimator(clf, X_test, y_test)\n1272 |         >>> plt.show()\n1273 |         \"\"\"\n1274 |         y_prob, pos_label, name = cls._validate_and_get_response_values(\n1275 |             estimator,\n1276 |             X,\n1277 |             y,\n1278 |             response_method=\"predict_proba\",\n1279 |             pos_label=pos_label,\n1280 |             name=name,\n1281 |         )\n1282 | \n1283 |         return cls.from_predictions(\n1284 |             y,\n1285 |             y_prob,\n1286 |             n_bins=n_bins,\n1287 |             strategy=strategy,\n1288 |             pos_label=pos_label,\n1289 |             name=name,\n1290 |             ref_line=ref_line,\n1291 |             ax=ax,\n1292 |             **kwargs,\n1293 |         )\n```\n\nFound function `sklearn/calibration.py:CalibrationDisplay.from_predictions`.\nSource: Retrieved entity using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n1296 | def from_predictions(\n1297 |         cls,\n1298 |         y_true,\n1299 |         y_prob,\n1300 |         *,\n1301 |         n_bins=5,\n1302 |         strategy=\"uniform\",\n1303 |         pos_label=None,\n1304 |         name=None,\n1305 |         ref_line=True,\n1306 |         ax=None,\n1307 |         **kwargs,\n1308 |     ):\n1309 |         \"\"\"Plot calibration curve using true labels and predicted probabilities.\n1310 | \n1311 |         Calibration curve, also known as reliability diagram, uses inputs\n1312 |     